.TH BT-SANE 1 "2026" "bt-sane" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bt-sane \- a saner Bluetooth desktop: a passive tray icon + an LE toggle
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bt-sane
is a small Bluetooth-UX suite: a passive, event-driven tray indicator that
replaces the CPU-hungry blueman-applet, and a controller-mode toggle. It is a
suite of tools installed on
.BR PATH ;
there is no eponymous
.B bt-sane
command.
.SH TOOLS
.TP
.B bt-indicator
A passive StatusNotifierItem tray icon for Bluetooth. It reads BlueZ once on the
system bus and subscribes to PropertiesChanged only on the adapter + paired
devices (never the LE advertisement flood), so it sits at ~0% idle. Left-click
connects/disconnects the last paired device, middle-click toggles LE (via
.BR bt-le ),
right-click opens
.BR blueman-manager .
Runs as a
.B --user
systemd daemon (installed by
.BR "setup.sh service" ).
.TP
.B bt-le
.B "bt-le on|off|status"
\(em flip the BlueZ
.I ControllerMode
between
.B dual
(LE on) and
.B bredr
(classic only), restarting bluetoothd.
.B status
is world-readable;
.BR on / off
edit
.I /etc/bluetooth/main.conf
and need root (an integrator installs bt-le to a root path with a narrow
NOPASSWD grant, which is how the tray's middle-click reaches it).
.SH RATIONALE
BlueZ defaults to
.B dual
mode; in an RF-dense room LE discovery floods blueman-applet with hundreds of
transient beacons and it busy-spins a CPU.
.B bredr
costs nothing when all your gear is classic audio, and
.B bt-le
flips back for a session that needs LE. The tray icon is passive by
construction, so it never spins.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BT_LE
path to the bt-le helper the tray invokes via sudo (default
.IR /usr/local/sbin/bt-le ).
.TP
.B BT_CONF
the BlueZ main.conf path (default
.IR /etc/bluetooth/main.conf ).
.TP
.B BT_INDICATOR_REENUM
re-enumeration interval tuning for the tray.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bluetoothctl (1),
.BR blueman-manager (1)
